Q: Type casting char pointer to be of type struct pointer A: <p>I think it is more safe. Instead of pointer punning I use <code>memcpy</code> for the struct and offset calculation for flexible array member reference</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;errno.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/types.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/inotify.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stddef.h&gt;

#define EVENT_SIZE  ( sizeof (struct inotify_event) )
#define BUF_LEN     ( 1024 * ( EVENT_SIZE + 16 ) )

size_t hdr = offsetof(struct inotify_event, name);

int main(void) 
{
  int length, i = 0;
  int fd;
  int wd;
  char buffer[BUF_LEN];

  fd = inotify_init();

  if ( fd &lt; 0 ) {
    perror( &quot;inotify_init&quot; );
  }

  wd = inotify_add_watch( fd, &quot;/home/strike&quot;, 
                         IN_MODIFY | IN_CREATE | IN_DELETE );

  length = read( fd, buffer, BUF_LEN );  

  if ( length &lt; 0 ) {
    perror( &quot;read&quot; );
  }  

  while ( i &lt; length ) {
    struct inotify_event event;
    memcpy(&amp;event,&amp;buffer[ i ], sizeof(event));
    char *name = &amp;buffer[ i + hdr];
    if ( event.len ) {
      if ( event.mask &amp; IN_CREATE ) {
        if ( event.mask &amp; IN_ISDIR ) {
          printf( &quot;The directory %s was created.\n&quot;, name );       
        }
        else {
          printf( &quot;The file %s was created.\n&quot;, name );
        }
      }
      else if ( event.mask &amp; IN_DELETE ) {
        if ( event.mask &amp; IN_ISDIR ) {
          printf( &quot;The directory %s was deleted.\n&quot;, name );       
        }
        else {
          printf( &quot;The file %s was deleted.\n&quot;, name );
        }
      }
      else if ( event.mask &amp; IN_MODIFY ) {
        if ( event.mask &amp; IN_ISDIR ) {
          printf( &quot;The directory %s was modified.\n&quot;, name );
        }
        else {
          printf( &quot;The file %s was modified.\n&quot;, name );
        }
      }
    }
    i += EVENT_SIZE + event.len;
  }

  ( void ) inotify_rm_watch( fd, wd );
  ( void ) close( fd );

  exit( 0 );
}
</code></pre>

Q: Return value type of class member accessor function to provide access to vector of unique_ptrs A: <p>If the vector of <code>Location</code> objects is set only once (say at construction), you have also the possibility to sort it with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/algorithm/ranges/sort.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges::sort</code></a> with the help of a projection yielding <code>Location::label</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class LocationDataBase {
 public:
    LocationDataBase (std::vector&lt;Location&gt;&amp;&amp; data) : location_data_(data) {
        std::ranges::sort (location_data_, {}, &amp;Location::label);
    };
 private:
    std::vector&lt;Location&gt; location_data_;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Since the vector is sorted, you can then have an efficient search of the first and last occurrence of an item as follows (we use again a projection on the <code>label</code> field)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::span&lt;Location&gt; GetObjectsAtLocation (std::string const&amp; label) {
    return std::span&lt;Location&gt; (
        std::ranges::lower_bound(location_data_, label, {}, &amp;Location::label),
        std::ranges::upper_bound(location_data_, label, {}, &amp;Location::label)
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note how one uses <code>std::ranges::lower_bound</code> and <code>std::ranges::upper_bound</code> and provides their result to build a <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/span.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::span</code></a> object; using a <code>std::span</code> involves no copy of <code>Location</code> objects since a <code>std::span</code> is a view on an already existing object (a <code>std::vector</code> in that use case).</p>
<p>By doing so, you need only one container (<code>location_data_</code>) that provides efficient search of <code>Location</code> objects given a label. This proposal requires C++23 because of usage of some <code>std::ranges</code> features.</p>
<p><strong>Potential drawback:</strong> This is just a proposal that allows to keep only one container. However, the way the lookup is done needs two lookups instead of one. It could be improved though to a certain extent and depending on your real use case, it might be not too costly.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/53YqK67We" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Update:</strong>
One advantage of this approach is it's quite easy to generalize to use another attribute from <code>Location</code> as a sorting criteria. One just adds a non type template parameter to <code>LocationDataBase</code> that takes a pointer to the member to be used as criteria like <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/W4h4cYvfd" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a></p>

Q: std::ranges::minmax slower than std::ranges::min and std::ranges::max A: <p>I think this is branch (mis-)prediction in action.</p>
<p>To achieve the required complexity, <code>ranges::minmax</code> processes two elements at a time, using the smaller of them for the <code>min</code> value and the other for the <code>max</code> value. The algorithm is roughly:</p>
<pre><code>auto min_val = vec[0];
auto max_val = min_val;
for (/* every two elements `elem1` and `elem2` in `vec` */) {
    if (elem2 &lt; elem1) {
        min_val = std::min(elem2, min_val);
        max_val = std::max(elem1, max_val);
    } else {
        min_val = std::min(elem1, min_val);
        max_val = std::max(elem2, max_val);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This costs 3 comparisons for every 2 elements: one for <code>elem2 &lt; elem1</code>, one for the <code>std::min</code> call, and one for the <code>std::max</code> call.</p>
<p>However, not all these comparisons are equal: the <code>std::min</code> and <code>std::max</code> calls are translated to (branchless) <code>minsd</code> and <code>maxsd</code> on x86-64, but the first comparison is translated to a conditional jump.</p>
<p>As elaborated in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/11227809/why-is-processing-a-sorted-array-faster-than-processing-an-unsorted-array">Why is processing a sorted array faster than processing an unsorted array?</a>, The performance of the conditional jump is subject to branch prediction; it's much faster when the outcome can be predicted.</p>
<p>In your benchmark, the vector is generated by a pseudo-random number generator. This makes it impossible for the processor to predict the outcome of <code>elem2 &lt; elem1</code> (and thus makes the conditional jump much slower than the extra comparison in the naive implementation). If I make the vector sorted, then <code>use_minmax</code> would be much faster than <code>use_min_and_max</code>.</p>
